  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist in Kibana. The rule has run once with status `ok`, which means it ran without errors and isn't firing right now. I couldn't confirm that its 5xx filter actually matches documents, because Kibana's console proxy is turned off here.\n\n**What the data holds:** each document in `kibana_sample_data_logs` is one web request. It records when it happened (`timestamp`), the response code (`response`), size in bytes, client IP, URL, source and destination country, OS, and user agent.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-eed22ab9 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels, at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-eed22ab9-web-logs-overview-a72aad53edda\n- **Totals:** total requests, unique visitors (counted by client IP), and total bytes served.\n- **Requests over time:** a bar chart split by the top 5 response codes.\n- **Response code breakdown:** a pie chart.\n- **Top 10 URLs:** a table with request count and bytes for each.\n- **Top 10 source countries:** a bar chart of requests.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-eed22ab9 5xx spike\"** (id `6eca4419-4dfa-418c-922f-1fa2b12e8580`)\n- It is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 requests in the last 5 minutes have a response code starting with \"5\".\n- It assumes `response.keyword` stores codes as text like \"503\". If it doesn't, the rule will never fire. You can check this by searching `response.keyword:5*` in Discover.\n- The rule is switched on but has no notification attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana. Add a connector (email, Slack, etc.) if you want to be told.",
